// New articles, 17 November 2026 (part 1): filling thinner subjects — the human body and the modern world.
ADDART({id:"joints",t:"Joints and Ligaments",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🦴",w:"Joint",r:["bones","muscles","spine","exercise","aging"],
p:[["Where bones meet","Joints are the places where two or more bones come together."],["Many kinds","Hinge, ball-and-socket and pivot joints allow different movements."],["Held together","Ligaments, cartilage and fluid keep joints strong and smooth."]],
l:"A joint is a place where two or more bones meet. The adult human body has around 350 joints, depending on how they are counted. Some joints, such as those between the bones of the skull, are fixed and barely move; others allow a little movement, like those between the vertebrae of the spine; and most, called synovial joints, move freely. Synovial joints are cushioned by smooth cartilage on the ends of the bones and lubricated by synovial fluid inside a capsule. Tough bands of tissue called ligaments connect bone to bone and keep joints stable, while tendons attach muscles to bones so they can pull and move them. Different shapes of joints allow different movements: hinges bend like a door, ball-and-socket joints rotate in many directions, and pivot joints twist. Joints can be injured by sprains and dislocations and can wear down with arthritis, but exercise, healthy weight and good posture help keep them working.",
s:[["Types of movable joints","Hinge joints, in the elbow, knee and fingers, bend and straighten in one direction. Ball-and-socket joints, in the shoulder and hip, let limbs swing and rotate. Pivot joints, such as the one between the top two vertebrae, let the head turn from side to side. Gliding joints in the wrist and ankle let bones slide past each other, and saddle joints, like the base of the thumb, move in two directions."],["Inside a joint","The ends of the bones are covered with articular cartilage, which is slippery and slightly squashy. The joint capsule is lined with a membrane that makes synovial fluid, a thick liquid like egg white that reduces friction. Some joints, like the knee, also have pads of cartilage called menisci that absorb shock."],["Ligaments and tendons","Ligaments are made of strong, slightly stretchy collagen fibers. A sprain happens when a ligament is stretched or torn, often at the ankle or knee. The anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) in the knee is a common sports injury. Tendons, such as the Achilles tendon at the back of the heel, connect muscles to bone."],["Joint problems","Osteoarthritis develops when cartilage wears away, causing pain and stiffness, especially in older people. Rheumatoid arthritis is an autoimmune disease in which the immune system attacks joint linings. Badly damaged hips and knees can be replaced with artificial joints made of metal, plastic and ceramic, one of the most successful operations in modern medicine."]],
k:[["Number of joints","About 350 in adults"],["Main movable type","Synovial joints"],["Bone-to-bone connectors","Ligaments"],["Muscle-to-bone connectors","Tendons"],["Common joint disease","Osteoarthritis"]],
d:[["c. 1890","First attempts at artificial hip joints"],["1962","John Charnley performs a modern hip replacement"],["1968","First modern knee replacements"],["1980s","Keyhole (arthroscopic) joint surgery spreads"],["2000s","Joint replacement becomes one of the most common operations"]],
s2:[["Why knuckles crack","When you pull or bend a joint, the pressure inside the fluid drops and a gas bubble forms quickly, making a popping sound. Studies have found no evidence that cracking your knuckles causes arthritis."],["Flexible bodies","Some people have joints that move further than usual, called hypermobility or being ‘double-jointed’. It is common in children and dancers and usually harmless, though in some conditions it can cause pain or dislocations."]],
sp:["Babies’ bones","Newborn babies have about 300 bones and soft spots in the skull called fontanelles, where the bones have not yet joined. As children grow, some bones fuse, leaving adults with 206 bones."],
g:[["Joint","A place where bones meet"],["Ligament","A tough band connecting bone to bone"],["Cartilage","Smooth, firm tissue covering bone ends"],["Arthritis","Inflammation and pain in joints"]],
f:["The knee is the largest joint in the body.","Babies have about 300 bones, adults 206.","Cracking your knuckles does not cause arthritis."],
fun:"The smallest joints in the body are between the three tiny bones of the middle ear, which pass sound vibrations to the inner ear.",
tl:"Ang kasukasuan ay lugar kung saan nagtatagpo ang dalawa o higit pang buto. Pinagdurugtong ang mga ito ng ligament at pinapakinis ng cartilage at likido, at may iba’t ibang uri tulad ng hinge at ball-and-socket."});

ADDART({id:"laughter",t:"Laughter",c:"Health and the Body",i:"😂",w:"Laughter",r:["brain","emotions","comedy","psychology","stress"],
p:[["A social signal","Laughter is a sound people make when something is funny or when they feel happy and connected."],["Older than language","Apes and even rats make laugh-like sounds during play."],["Good for you","Laughing can relieve stress and help people bond."]],
l:"Laughter is a natural response made of rhythmic sounds and breathing, often with smiling and body movements, usually triggered by humor, play, tickling or relief. Scientists think laughter evolved long before language, because great apes laugh during play and tickling, and rats make high-pitched ‘laughing’ chirps when tickled. Research shows that people laugh up to 30 times more often when they are with others than when alone, and that most laughter in conversation follows ordinary remarks rather than jokes, suggesting its main job is social: signaling friendliness, safety and belonging. Laughter is also contagious; hearing it activates areas of the brain that prepare us to laugh too. Laughing triggers the release of endorphins, the body’s natural feel-good chemicals, and can temporarily reduce stress and raise pain tolerance. Different cultures laugh at different things, but the sound of laughter is recognized everywhere.",
s:[["How laughing works","When we laugh, the diaphragm and chest muscles contract in short bursts, pushing air out in a series of ‘ha-ha’ sounds about a fifth of a second apart. The face forms a wide smile, the eyes may squint and water, and the whole body may shake. Strong laughter can make it hard to stand or breathe."],["Why we laugh","The psychologist Robert Provine found that speakers laugh more than listeners and that only about one in ten laughs follows something meant to be funny. Laughter helps groups bond, eases tension and shows that a situation is not a threat. Babies begin laughing at around three to four months."],["Theories of humor","Philosophers have offered several explanations for why things are funny. The superiority theory says we laugh at others’ misfortunes; the relief theory says laughter releases nervous energy; and the incongruity theory, most popular today, says we laugh when something unexpected breaks a pattern in a harmless way."],["Laughter and health","Laughing with others is linked to lower stress hormones and better mood. In one experiment, people who had just watched a comedy could hold their arms in an uncomfortable position longer than those who watched a documentary. Laughter yoga clubs, started in India in 1995, use deliberate laughing as exercise."]],
k:[["Main purpose","Social bonding and signaling"],["Babies first laugh","At about 3–4 months"],["More common in company","Up to 30 times more than alone"],["Chemicals released","Endorphins"],["Laughter yoga founded","1995, Mumbai"]],
d:[["c. 1900 BC","One of the oldest recorded jokes, from ancient Sumer"],["1872","Darwin writes about laughter in animals and humans"],["1962","Laughter epidemic in Tanganyika"],["1995","First laughter yoga club in Mumbai"],["2000","Robert Provine publishes Laughter: A Scientific Investigation"]],
s2:[["Tickling","You cannot tickle yourself well because your brain predicts the touch and dampens the response. Ticklish laughter is partly a reflex and is shared with other apes, which laugh when tickled by their companions."],["The 1962 laughter epidemic","In 1962, uncontrollable laughter spread among schoolgirls in a boarding school in Tanganyika (now Tanzania) and then to nearby villages, affecting about 1,000 people over several months. Researchers think it was a form of mass psychogenic illness linked to stress."]],
sp:["Laughing rats","In the 1990s, neuroscientist Jaak Panksepp discovered that rats make ultrasonic chirps, too high for humans to hear, when tickled or playing. Tickled rats chased the scientist’s hand to be tickled again, suggesting that playful joy is an ancient emotion."],
g:[["Diaphragm","The muscle under the lungs that helps us breathe"],["Endorphins","Natural chemicals that reduce pain and improve mood"],["Contagious","Spreading easily from person to person"],["Incongruity","Something that doesn’t fit what we expect"]],
f:["Rats laugh when tickled, but too high-pitched for us to hear.","Most laughter follows ordinary remarks, not jokes.","You can’t tickle yourself because your brain predicts the touch."],
fun:"One of the world’s oldest recorded jokes, from ancient Sumer around 1900 BC, is a rude joke about a young woman and her husband, showing that people have always loved silly humor.",
tl:"Ang pagtawa ay likas na tugon kapag may nakakatawa o kapag masaya at malapit tayo sa iba. Mas matanda ito kaysa wika, dahil tumatawa rin ang mga unggoy at daga kapag naglalaro, at nakababawas ito ng stress."});

ADDART({id:"autism",t:"Autism",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🧩",w:"Autism",r:["brain","mental","psychology","dyslexia","antivax"],
p:[["A different way of thinking","Autism is a developmental condition that affects how people communicate, interact and experience the world."],["A spectrum","Autistic people differ widely in their strengths and the support they need."],["Common","About 1 in 31 children in the United States is diagnosed with autism."]],
l:"Autism, or autism spectrum disorder (ASD), is a lifelong developmental condition that affects how a person communicates, interacts with others and experiences the world. Autistic people may find social communication, such as reading facial expressions or understanding unwritten social rules, more difficult; often prefer routines and predictability; may have intense, focused interests; and may be very sensitive, or less sensitive, to sounds, lights, textures and other sensations. Because autism varies so much, it is called a spectrum: some autistic people speak fluently and live independently, while others speak little or not at all and need daily support, and many have particular strengths such as attention to detail or pattern recognition. Autism is strongly influenced by genetics, and research has found no link with vaccines. Diagnoses have increased as awareness and definitions have broadened; in the United States, about 1 in 31 eight-year-olds was identified as autistic in data published in 2025.",
s:[["History","In 1943 the psychiatrist Leo Kanner described children with ‘autistic disturbances of affective contact’, and in 1944 Hans Asperger described children with similar traits who spoke well. For decades, autism was wrongly blamed on cold parenting. Since 2013, the main US diagnostic manual has grouped several related conditions into a single ‘autism spectrum disorder’."],["Causes","Twin and family studies show that genes play the largest role, with hundreds of genes involved. Some factors during pregnancy, such as very premature birth, may slightly raise the likelihood. A 1998 study claiming a link with the MMR vaccine was found to be fraudulent, and very large studies have found no connection. In 2025, claims by US officials linking autism to paracetamol use in pregnancy were disputed by many scientists and medical organizations."],["Diagnosis and support","Signs can often be noticed in the first two to three years, such as limited eye contact, delayed speech or repetitive movements, but many people, especially girls and women, are diagnosed later in life. Support may include speech and occupational therapy, visual schedules, quiet spaces and adjustments at school and work."],["Neurodiversity","Many autistic people see autism as a natural variation in how brains work, part of human neurodiversity, rather than something to be cured. They emphasize acceptance, accessibility and the right to be involved in decisions about autism research and services. Others, especially families of people with high support needs, stress the challenges and the need for more help."]],
k:[["Full name","Autism spectrum disorder (ASD)"],["First described","1943 (Kanner) and 1944 (Asperger)"],["US prevalence (2025 report)","About 1 in 31 eight-year-olds"],["Main cause","Mostly genetic"],["Vaccine link","None found"],["World Autism Awareness Day","2 April"]],
d:[["1943","Leo Kanner describes autism"],["1944","Hans Asperger describes similar children"],["1998","Fraudulent MMR–autism paper published"],["2007","UN declares World Autism Awareness Day"],["2013","Autism spectrum disorder defined as one diagnosis"],["2025","US report finds 1 in 31 children identified"]],
s2:[["Sensory experiences","Many autistic people experience sensations more intensely, so busy shops, loud hand dryers or scratchy clothing can be overwhelming. Some stores and museums now offer ‘quiet hours’ with dimmer lights and less noise, and noise-reducing headphones help many people."],["Stimming","Repetitive movements or sounds, such as hand-flapping, rocking or humming, are called stimming. Autistic people often explain that stimming helps them manage emotions and sensory input, and experts now generally advise against stopping harmless stimming."]],
sp:["Temple Grandin","Temple Grandin, an autistic woman who did not speak until she was about four, became a professor of animal science and designed humane livestock facilities used across North America. Her books and talks helped change how people understand autism."],
g:[["Spectrum","A wide range of different forms"],["Neurodiversity","The natural variety of human brains and minds"],["Diagnosis","Identifying a condition"],["Stimming","Repetitive movements or sounds that help self-regulation"]],
f:["Autism is a lifelong condition, not an illness that can be caught.","Research has found no link between vaccines and autism.","Many autistic people are diagnosed as adults."],
fun:"Temple Grandin invented a ‘hug machine’ that applies gentle pressure, which she found calming; similar weighted blankets are now widely used.",
tl:"Ang autism ay kondisyong pang-develop na nakaaapekto sa pakikipag-usap, pakikisalamuha at pagdanas ng mundo. Tinatawag itong spectrum dahil iba-iba ang kalakasan at pangangailangan ng mga taong autistic, at walang kaugnayan ito sa mga bakuna."});

ADDART({id:"commoncold",t:"The Common Cold",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🤧",w:"Common cold",r:["immune","flu","hygiene","antibiotics","fever"],
p:[["The most common illness","The common cold is a mild viral infection of the nose and throat."],["Over 200 viruses","Rhinoviruses cause most colds, which is why there is no vaccine."],["Rest and time","Colds usually get better on their own in about a week."]],
l:"The common cold is a mild viral infection of the upper respiratory tract, mainly the nose and throat. It is the most frequent illness in humans: adults catch two to three colds a year on average, and young children often catch even more. More than 200 different viruses can cause colds, most commonly rhinoviruses, as well as some coronaviruses and other viruses. Symptoms include a runny or blocked nose, sneezing, sore throat, cough and sometimes a mild fever, and usually last about a week to ten days. Colds spread through droplets from coughs and sneezes and by touching surfaces and then the eyes, nose or mouth. Because so many viruses are involved and they change, there is no vaccine, and antibiotics do not work against viruses. Rest, drinking fluids and medicines that relieve symptoms help people feel better, while handwashing is the best protection.",
s:[["Causes","Rhinoviruses, which grow best at the slightly cooler temperatures inside the nose, cause about a third to a half of colds. There are more than 160 types of rhinovirus alone. Catching one type gives some immunity to it but not to the others, so people can catch colds again and again."],["Symptoms","A cold usually starts with a scratchy throat, followed by sneezing and a runny nose. Mucus may turn thicker and yellow or green as the immune system fights the infection, which does not necessarily mean a bacterial infection. Most symptoms are caused by the body’s immune response rather than by the virus itself."],["Treatment","Rest, fluids, honey for coughs in children over one year old, saltwater gargles and pain relievers can ease symptoms. Antibiotics should not be used for colds, because they do not kill viruses and overuse helps bacteria become resistant. Evidence for remedies such as vitamin C and zinc is mixed."],["Myths","Being cold or having wet hair does not by itself cause a cold, though colds are more common in winter, when people spend more time indoors together and dry air may help viruses spread. The old saying ‘feed a cold, starve a fever’ has no scientific basis; people should eat and drink normally when sick."]],
k:[["Cause","Viruses, mostly rhinoviruses"],["Number of possible viruses","Over 200"],["Usual length","About 7–10 days"],["Adults per year","2–3 colds on average"],["Vaccine","None"]],
d:[["1914","Scientists show colds are caused by an infectious agent"],["1946","Britain opens the Common Cold Unit"],["1956","Rhinoviruses first identified"],["1960s","Coronaviruses identified as a cause of colds"],["1990","Common Cold Unit closes"]],
s2:[["Colds and flu","Flu, caused by influenza viruses, usually starts suddenly with high fever, aches and exhaustion, while colds are milder and come on gradually. Flu can be dangerous, and yearly vaccines protect against it; colds rarely cause serious illness."],["How to avoid colds","Washing hands with soap, avoiding touching the face, covering coughs and sneezes, and staying home when sick reduce the spread of colds. Good sleep and a healthy lifestyle help the immune system."]],
sp:["The Common Cold Unit","From 1946 to 1990, Britain’s Common Cold Unit in Salisbury invited volunteers to stay for 10 days, free of charge, to be deliberately given colds for research. About 20,000 people took part. Although no cure was found, the unit learned much about how colds spread."],
g:[["Virus","A tiny germ that can only multiply inside living cells"],["Rhinovirus","The most common type of cold virus"],["Respiratory tract","The nose, throat, airways and lungs"],["Antibiotic","A medicine that kills bacteria, not viruses"]],
f:["More than 200 different viruses can cause a cold.","Antibiotics do not work on colds.","Rhinovirus means ‘nose virus’."],
fun:"Volunteers at Britain’s Common Cold Unit were given a free 10-day holiday in the countryside, and some couples who met there later married.",
tl:"Ang sipon ay banayad na impeksyon ng virus sa ilong at lalamunan, at ito ang pinakakaraniwang sakit ng tao. Mahigit 200 virus ang maaaring magdulot nito, kaya walang bakuna, at kusa itong gumagaling sa mga isang linggo."});

ADDART({id:"alzheimers",t:"Alzheimer’s Disease and Dementia",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🧠",w:"Alzheimer's disease",r:["brain","memory","aging","agingworld","mental"],
p:[["Memory loss","Dementia is a decline in memory and thinking that affects daily life."],["The most common cause","Alzheimer’s disease causes most cases of dementia."],["A growing challenge","About 57 million people worldwide live with dementia."]],
l:"Dementia is a general term for a decline in memory, thinking, language and judgment severe enough to interfere with daily life. It is not a normal part of aging, although it becomes much more common in older age. Alzheimer’s disease is the most common cause, responsible for an estimated 60 to 70 percent of cases; others include vascular dementia, caused by reduced blood flow to the brain, and dementia with Lewy bodies. In Alzheimer’s disease, abnormal clumps of a protein called amyloid build up between brain cells, and tangles of another protein, tau, form inside them, gradually killing neurons, beginning in areas important for memory. The World Health Organization estimated that about 57 million people had dementia in 2021, a number expected to rise as populations age. There is no cure, but new medicines approved since 2023 can modestly slow early Alzheimer’s, and healthy habits may reduce the risk of dementia.",
s:[["Discovery","In 1906, the German psychiatrist Alois Alzheimer described the case of Auguste Deter, a woman in her fifties with memory loss and confusion. After her death, he found plaques and tangles in her brain under the microscope. The disease was later named after him."],["Symptoms and stages","Early signs include forgetting recent events, repeating questions and getting lost in familiar places. Over years, people may have trouble speaking, recognizing loved ones and caring for themselves, and may experience mood changes. In the late stages they need full-time care."],["Treatment and research","Medicines first approved in the 1990s can ease some symptoms for a time. In 2023 and 2024, US regulators approved lecanemab and donanemab, antibody drugs that remove amyloid and modestly slow decline in early Alzheimer’s, though they carry risks such as brain swelling. Blood tests to help diagnose Alzheimer’s are being introduced."],["Reducing risk","A 2024 commission in The Lancet estimated that nearly half of dementia cases might be prevented or delayed by addressing risk factors such as high blood pressure, hearing loss, smoking, physical inactivity, high cholesterol, social isolation, poor vision and air pollution. Staying physically, mentally and socially active is recommended."]],
k:[["People with dementia (2021)","About 57 million"],["Share caused by Alzheimer’s","About 60–70%"],["First described","1906, Alois Alzheimer"],["Key proteins","Amyloid and tau"],["World Alzheimer’s Day","21 September"]],
d:[["1906","Alois Alzheimer describes the disease"],["1984","Amyloid protein identified in plaques"],["1994","World Alzheimer’s Day first held"],["1993","First drug to ease Alzheimer’s symptoms approved in the USA"],["2023","Lecanemab fully approved in the USA"],["2024","Donanemab approved; Lancet report on preventable risk"]],
s2:[["Caring for people with dementia","Most people with dementia are cared for at home by family members, often for many years. Dementia-friendly communities train shop staff, bus drivers and others to help people with memory problems, and memory cafés offer social support."],["Music and memory","Music memories are often preserved even in later stages of dementia. Playing familiar songs can calm people, spark memories and help them connect with loved ones, and singing groups are used in many care homes."]],
sp:["The Nun Study","Since 1986, researchers have studied hundreds of elderly nuns in the United States who donated their brains to science. Some nuns whose brains showed many plaques had no symptoms of dementia, suggesting that education, mental activity and positive emotions may help the brain cope with disease."],
g:[["Dementia","A decline in memory and thinking that affects daily life"],["Neuron","A nerve cell in the brain"],["Amyloid","A protein that forms plaques in Alzheimer’s disease"],["Caregiver","A person who looks after someone who needs help"]],
f:["Dementia is not a normal part of aging.","Alzheimer’s disease causes most cases of dementia.","Music memories often survive late into dementia."],
fun:"Auguste Deter’s original medical files and brain slides were rediscovered in Frankfurt in the 1990s, almost 90 years after Alzheimer studied them.",
tl:"Ang dementia ay paghina ng memorya at pag-iisip na nakaaapekto sa araw-araw na buhay, at ang Alzheimer’s disease ang pinakakaraniwang sanhi nito. Mga 57 milyong tao sa mundo ang may dementia, at wala pang lunas, ngunit may mga bagong gamot na nakapagpapabagal nito."});

ADDART({id:"cholera",t:"Cholera",c:"Health and the Body",i:"💧",w:"Cholera",r:["germtheory","sanitation","waterscarcity","pandemic","antibiotics"],
p:[["A waterborne disease","Cholera is an infection of the intestines spread by contaminated water and food."],["Deadly but treatable","It can kill within hours, but simple rehydration saves most patients."],["Clean water stops it","Cholera disappears where people have clean water and sanitation."]],
l:"Cholera is an acute infection of the intestine caused by the bacterium Vibrio cholerae, which spreads through water or food contaminated with the feces of an infected person. It causes severe watery diarrhea and vomiting that can lead to dehydration and death within hours if untreated. Yet most patients can be saved with oral rehydration solution, a simple mixture of clean water, sugar and salts, and severe cases with intravenous fluids and antibiotics. Cholera spread from the Ganges delta to the rest of the world in the 19th century in a series of pandemics that killed millions. In 1854, the London doctor John Snow showed that cholera was spread by contaminated water, a founding moment of modern epidemiology. Cholera has disappeared from places with clean water and sanitation but still affects communities affected by poverty, war and disasters, with the World Health Organization estimating millions of cases each year.",
s:[["History","Cholera existed in South Asia for centuries. From 1817, trade and troop movements carried it around the world in seven pandemics; the seventh, which began in Indonesia in 1961, continues today. Outbreaks terrified 19th-century cities, killing rich and poor alike and spurring the building of sewers and clean water systems."],["John Snow and the pump","During an outbreak in London’s Soho in 1854, John Snow mapped the cases and found they clustered around a water pump on Broad Street. When the pump’s handle was removed, the outbreak, which was already declining, ended. Snow’s map became a famous example of using data to trace disease."],["The bacterium","The Italian scientist Filippo Pacini identified the cholera bacterium in 1854, but his work was overlooked; Robert Koch identified it again in 1883. The bacterium releases a toxin that makes the intestines pour out water and salts."],["Cholera today","Large outbreaks have occurred in Haiti after the 2010 earthquake, in Yemen during its civil war, and in parts of Africa and Asia. Oral cholera vaccines provide several years of protection and are used in outbreaks, but global supplies have been short. Clean water, sanitation and hygiene are the long-term solution."]],
k:[["Cause","Bacterium Vibrio cholerae"],["Spread by","Contaminated water and food"],["Main treatment","Oral rehydration solution"],["Ongoing pandemic","Seventh pandemic, since 1961"],["Famous investigator","John Snow, 1854"]],
d:[["1817","First cholera pandemic begins"],["1854","John Snow traces the Broad Street outbreak"],["1883","Robert Koch identifies the cholera bacterium"],["1961","Seventh pandemic begins"],["1960s–70s","Oral rehydration therapy developed"],["2010","Cholera outbreak in Haiti"],["2016","Large outbreak begins in Yemen"]],
s2:[["Oral rehydration solution","Developed in Bangladesh and India in the 1960s and 1970s, oral rehydration solution lets the gut absorb water even during cholera. The medical journal The Lancet called it potentially the most important medical advance of the 20th century, because it has saved millions of lives, especially children with diarrhea."],["Cities and sewers","Fear of cholera helped drive great engineering projects, such as London’s sewer system built by Joseph Bazalgette after the ‘Great Stink’ of 1858. Clean water and sewers did more to improve health in the 19th century than any medicine."]],
sp:["The Broad Street pump","A replica of the Broad Street pump, without a handle, stands in Soho, London, near a pub named after John Snow. Public health experts sometimes hold ceremonies removing a pump handle to celebrate disease prevention."],
g:[["Dehydration","Loss of too much water from the body"],["Epidemiology","The study of how diseases spread in populations"],["Sanitation","Systems for safely removing human waste"],["Pandemic","A disease outbreak spreading across many countries"]],
f:["Cholera can kill within hours but is easily treated with rehydration.","John Snow’s map is a famous example of data saving lives.","The seventh cholera pandemic has continued since 1961."],
fun:"John Snow was also a pioneer of anesthesia and gave chloroform to Queen Victoria during the birth of two of her children.",
tl:"Ang kolera ay impeksyon sa bituka na kumakalat sa maruming tubig at pagkain. Maaari itong pumatay sa loob ng ilang oras, ngunit karamihan sa pasyente ay naliligtas ng simpleng rehydration, at nawawala ito kung may malinis na tubig at sanitasyon."});

ADDART({id:"fandom",t:"Fandoms and Fan Culture",c:"The Modern World",i:"🎤",w:"Fandom",r:["kpop","anime","cosplay","socialmedia","superhero"],
p:[["Shared passion","A fandom is a community of fans devoted to a show, band, book, game or sports team."],["Creative fans","Fans write stories, make art and organize events."],["Powerful online","Social media has made fandoms larger and more influential than ever."]],
l:"A fandom is a community of fans who share enthusiasm for something, such as a television series, film franchise, book series, musician, video game or sports team. Fans do more than watch or listen: they discuss theories, collect merchandise, travel to concerts and conventions, make fan art and fan fiction, dress up as characters in cosplay, and form friendships with people around the world. Organized fandom dates back at least to the 19th century, when readers of Sherlock Holmes stories reacted with dismay to the detective’s apparent death in 1893, and grew with science fiction clubs, Beatlemania and Star Trek conventions in the 20th century. The internet and social media transformed fandoms into global networks that can trend topics, raise money for charity and influence what companies make. Fandoms can create belonging and creativity, but can also show darker sides, such as harassment of critics, celebrities or other fans.",
s:[["Early fandoms","When Arthur Conan Doyle killed off Sherlock Holmes in 1893, thousands of readers cancelled subscriptions to the magazine that published the stories, and he eventually brought Holmes back. Science fiction fans organized clubs and fanzines from the 1930s, and the first World Science Fiction Convention was held in New York in 1939."],["Conventions","Star Trek fans held their first large convention in New York in 1972 and campaigned successfully to save the show from cancellation in 1968. San Diego Comic-Con, started in 1970 with about 300 people, now attracts well over 100,000 visitors. Anime, gaming and K-pop conventions are held around the world."],["Fan creativity","Fan fiction imagines new adventures for favorite characters, and websites host millions of fan stories. Some famous authors began by writing fan fiction. Fans also create art, videos, music remixes, translations of foreign shows and wikis that document every detail of their favorite worlds."],["Fandom online","K-pop fandoms, such as BTS’s ARMY, coordinate streaming, voting and charity drives across countries. Sports fans follow teams worldwide through online communities. When fans feel strongly, they can pressure studios to change plans, as when fans persuaded a studio to redesign Sonic the Hedgehog for a 2020 film."]],
k:[["Meaning","A community of devoted fans"],["Early example","Sherlock Holmes readers, 1893"],["First Worldcon","1939, New York"],["San Diego Comic-Con founded","1970"],["Fan activities","Fan fiction, fan art, cosplay, conventions"]],
d:[["1893","Readers protest the ‘death’ of Sherlock Holmes"],["1939","First World Science Fiction Convention"],["1964","Beatlemania sweeps the United States"],["1970","San Diego Comic-Con begins"],["1972","First large Star Trek convention"],["2010s","K-pop fandoms grow worldwide online"]],
s2:[["Sports fans","Sports fandom is one of the oldest and largest. Fans wear team colors, sing chants and travel long distances to support their teams. Studies suggest that supporting a team can give people a strong sense of identity and community, and even affect mood after wins and losses."],["Toxic fandom","Sometimes fans harass actors, writers or critics when they dislike a story choice, or attack fans of rivals online. Many fandom communities create rules and campaigns to keep spaces welcoming and to separate passion from abuse."]],
sp:["The word ‘fan’","The word fan is probably short for ‘fanatic’ and was first used in the late 19th century for enthusiastic baseball supporters in the United States. Fandom combines ‘fan’ with ‘-dom’, as in kingdom."],
g:[["Fandom","A community of fans"],["Fan fiction","Stories written by fans using existing characters"],["Convention","A large gathering of fans"],["Merchandise","Products such as shirts and toys linked to a brand"]],
f:["Readers made Arthur Conan Doyle bring Sherlock Holmes back.","San Diego Comic-Con began with about 300 people.","‘Fan’ probably comes from ‘fanatic’."],
fun:"After the 1968 fan letter-writing campaign that saved Star Trek, NASA named its first space shuttle test vehicle Enterprise in 1976, partly because of fan petitions.",
tl:"Ang fandom ay komunidad ng mga tagahanga ng isang palabas, banda, aklat, laro o koponan. Gumagawa sila ng fan art, kuwento at mga pagtitipon, at pinalaki ng social media ang kanilang impluwensiya sa buong mundo."});

ADDART({id:"emoji",t:"Emoji",c:"The Modern World",i:"😀",w:"Emoji",r:["smartphone","socialmedia","writing","memes","langevo"],
p:[["Pictures in messages","Emoji are small pictures used in text messages and online."],["Born in Japan","The first popular set was designed in Japan in 1999."],["A global language?","Nearly 4,000 emoji are now part of the Unicode standard."]],
l:"Emoji are small digital images, such as smiling faces, hearts, animals, foods and flags, used in text messages, emails and social media to express emotions and ideas. The word comes from Japanese e (‘picture’) and moji (‘character’) and is not related to ‘emotion’, despite the similarity. Emoji became popular in Japan in the late 1990s; in 1999, designer Shigetaka Kurita created a set of 176 simple emoji for the mobile internet service of the company NTT Docomo. After Apple and Google pushed for them, emoji were added to Unicode, the international standard for computer text, in 2010, allowing them to work across devices and countries. New emoji are proposed by the public and approved by the Unicode Consortium each year; by 2026, the standard included nearly 4,000. Emoji help add tone to short messages, though their meanings can vary between cultures and generations, and people sometimes misread them.",
s:[["Origins","Before emoji, people made ‘emoticons’ from keyboard characters, such as :-), first proposed by computer scientist Scott Fahlman in 1982. Japanese mobile phones in the 1990s introduced picture characters. Kurita’s 176 emoji, each just 12 by 12 pixels, were inspired by weather symbols, manga and street signs."],["Unicode","Unicode gives every character a number so that text appears the same on any device. Emoji were included in 2010, and Apple’s iPhone keyboard made them popular worldwide in 2011. Each company draws its own versions, so the same emoji can look slightly different on different phones."],["Adding new emoji","Anyone can propose a new emoji by explaining why it is needed and likely to be used. Additions have included skin tone options (2015), more professions and genders, foods from many cultures, and accessibility symbols such as wheelchairs and hearing aids. Proposals for specific brands or people are not accepted."],["Meanings","Some emoji gain new meanings online; the skull emoji is often used to mean ‘I’m dying of laughter’, and the thumbs-up can seem curt to younger users. In a few countries, courts have considered emoji as evidence of meaning in contracts or threats. Linguists study emoji as a kind of gesture that adds tone to writing."]],
k:[["Word origin","Japanese e (picture) + moji (character)"],["First popular set","1999, Shigetaka Kurita, 176 emoji"],["Added to Unicode","2010"],["Number in the standard","Nearly 4,000 (2026)"],["World Emoji Day","17 July"]],
d:[["1982","Scott Fahlman proposes the :-) emoticon"],["1999","Kurita designs 176 emoji in Japan"],["2010","Emoji added to Unicode"],["2011","iPhone emoji keyboard available worldwide"],["2015","Skin tone options added; ‘Face with Tears of Joy’ named word of the year"],["2016","MoMA acquires Kurita’s original emoji"]],
s2:[["Most popular emoji","For years, ‘Face with Tears of Joy’ 😂 was the most used emoji in the world, and Oxford Dictionaries chose it as its 2015 word of the year. Hearts, laughing faces and thumbs-up are also among the most used."],["World Emoji Day","World Emoji Day is celebrated on 17 July because the calendar emoji on Apple devices shows that date, which was the date Apple announced its iCal calendar app in 2002."]],
sp:["Emoji in a museum","In 2016, the Museum of Modern Art in New York added Kurita’s original 176 emoji to its permanent collection, recognizing them as an important piece of design history."],
g:[["Emoji","A small picture used in digital messages"],["Emoticon","A face made from keyboard characters, like :-)"],["Unicode","The international standard for digital text characters"],["Pixel","A tiny dot that makes up a digital image"]],
f:["‘Emoji’ is not related to the word ‘emotion’.","The first popular emoji were only 12 by 12 pixels.","A Unicode emoji looks different on different companies’ phones."],
fun:"Herman Melville’s novel Moby-Dick has been ‘translated’ entirely into emoji in a crowdsourced project called Emoji Dick, which the US Library of Congress added to its collection.",
tl:"Ang emoji ay maliliit na larawang ginagamit sa text at online upang ipahayag ang damdamin. Nagsimula ito sa Japan noong 1999, at halos 4,000 na ang emoji sa pamantayang Unicode."});

ADDART({id:"realitytv",t:"Reality Television",c:"The Modern World",i:"📺",w:"Reality television",r:["tv","influencers","streaming","comedy","fandom"],
p:[["Real people on screen","Reality TV shows ordinary people or celebrities in unscripted situations."],["Many formats","Formats include competitions, talent shows and documentaries of daily life."],["Popular and criticized","Viewers love the drama, but critics question how ‘real’ it is."]],
l:"Reality television is a genre of TV programs that show people, often ordinary members of the public, in situations that are presented as unscripted. Formats include competitions such as Survivor, talent shows such as the Idol and Got Talent series, cooking contests, dating shows, home makeover programs and ‘docusoaps’ following the lives of families or celebrities. Early forerunners included Candid Camera in 1948 and the documentary series An American Family in 1973, but the genre exploded in the late 1990s and 2000s with Big Brother, first broadcast in the Netherlands in 1999, and Survivor in 2000. Reality shows are cheap to make compared with dramas, can attract huge audiences and are sold as formats around the world, with local versions in dozens of countries. Critics argue that producers manipulate events through editing and setups, that shows encourage humiliation or conflict, and that contestants can suffer mental health harm, leading to new duty-of-care rules.",
s:[["Origins","Candid Camera filmed ordinary people reacting to pranks from 1948. An American Family (1973) followed the Loud family for months, including the parents’ divorce, sparking debate about privacy. MTV’s The Real World (1992) put young strangers in a house together and filmed their lives."],["The boom","Big Brother, created by the Dutch company Endemol in 1999, locked contestants in a house watched by cameras, with viewers voting people out. Survivor, Pop Idol and Who Wants to Be a Millionaire? became global formats. Shows made unknown people famous overnight; singers Kelly Clarkson and Carrie Underwood were discovered on American Idol."],["How ‘real’ is it?","Most reality shows are carefully planned. Producers choose contestants with strong personalities, set up challenges and situations, and edit hours of footage into dramatic stories, sometimes creating ‘villains’ and ‘heroes’. Some scenes are re-filmed or prompted, though true scripts are usually avoided."],["Wellbeing concerns","After the deaths of several former contestants in Britain and elsewhere, broadcasters introduced psychological screening, aftercare and rules on how contestants are treated. In 2021, the UK regulator Ofcom strengthened protections for people taking part in programs."]],
k:[["Early example","Candid Camera, 1948"],["First ‘docusoap’","An American Family, 1973"],["Big Brother first aired","1999, Netherlands"],["Survivor (US) began","2000"],["Common formats","Competitions, talent shows, dating, makeovers"]],
d:[["1948","Candid Camera begins"],["1973","An American Family airs"],["1992","The Real World premieres"],["1999","Big Brother debuts in the Netherlands"],["2000","Survivor begins in the USA"],["2001","Pop Idol launches in the UK"],["2021","UK strengthens rules to protect participants"]],
s2:[["A global business","Reality formats are among the most traded TV products in the world. A single format, such as Got Talent or MasterChef, may have versions in more than 50 countries, adapted to local culture but following the same rules and look."],["From reality TV to influencers","Reality shows helped create a new kind of celebrity, famous for being themselves rather than for acting or singing. Many former contestants now work as social media influencers, and social media has become a kind of self-produced reality show."]],
sp:["The Truman Show","The 1998 film The Truman Show imagined a man who does not know his whole life is a TV show watched by millions. Released just before the reality TV boom, it seemed to predict the genre and remains a popular way to discuss privacy and media."],
g:[["Genre","A category of film, TV or literature"],["Unscripted","Without written lines for the people on screen"],["Format","A show’s concept and rules, sold to other countries"],["Duty of care","A responsibility to protect people from harm"]],
f:["Big Brother was first broadcast in the Netherlands in 1999.","Reality shows are much cheaper to make than dramas.","Kelly Clarkson became famous by winning American Idol."],
fun:"The name Big Brother comes from George Orwell’s novel Nineteen Eighty-Four, in which an all-seeing ruler watches citizens through screens.",
tl:"Ang reality TV ay mga palabas na nagpapakita ng ordinaryong tao o artista sa mga sitwasyong hindi iskrip. Popular ito dahil sa drama, ngunit pinupuna kung gaano ito katotoo at kung paano tinatrato ang mga kalahok."});

ADDART({id:"themeparks",t:"Theme Parks",c:"The Modern World",i:"🎢",w:"Amusement park",r:["overtourism","cinema","anim","machines","tourism"],
p:[["Rides and stories","Theme parks are amusement parks built around stories, characters or themes."],["From pleasure gardens","They grew from old pleasure gardens and fairs."],["Disneyland changed everything","Disneyland, opened in 1955, created the modern theme park."]],
l:"A theme park is an amusement park whose rides, buildings and attractions are designed around a theme, such as fairy tales, films, history or adventure. Amusement parks grew from European pleasure gardens and fairs: Bakken in Denmark, dating from 1583, is considered the oldest operating amusement park, and Copenhagen’s Tivoli Gardens opened in 1843. In the late 19th century, Coney Island in New York became famous for roller coasters and electric lights. Walt Disney opened Disneyland in California on 17 July 1955, combining rides with themed ‘lands’, characters and storytelling, and created the model for modern theme parks. Today, parks run by Disney, Universal and others in the United States, Europe and Asia attract tens of millions of visitors a year, while engineers build ever taller, faster and more inventive rides. Parks are major tourist attractions and employers, but critics point to high prices, crowding and environmental costs.",
s:[["From gardens to parks","Pleasure gardens such as Vauxhall in London (from 1661) offered music, food and fireworks. World’s fairs introduced new attractions: the first Ferris wheel was built for the 1893 Chicago fair. Coney Island’s parks, from 1895, offered roller coasters, water chutes and dazzling lights."],["Disneyland","Disneyland was divided into themed areas, such as Main Street, U.S.A., Fantasyland and Tomorrowland, with a castle at its center. Its opening day was chaotic, with heat, fake tickets and soft new asphalt, but it soon became a huge success. Walt Disney World in Florida opened in 1971 and is the most visited resort in the world."],["Roller coasters","Roller coasters evolved from Russian ice slides of the 17th century. Steel coasters from the 1950s allowed loops and twists. Today’s record holders include Formula Rossa in Abu Dhabi, which reaches about 240 kilometers per hour, and very tall coasters over 130 meters high. Engineers carefully calculate forces to keep rides thrilling but safe."],["Theme parks today","Major parks now include immersive areas based on films and books, such as Harry Potter worlds, and use technology such as virtual reality and robotic animatronics. Asia has become a fast-growing market, with large parks in China, Japan and Singapore. Water parks and safari parks are related attractions."]],
k:[["Oldest operating amusement park","Bakken, Denmark (1583)"],["Tivoli Gardens opened","1843, Copenhagen"],["Disneyland opened","17 July 1955"],["First Ferris wheel","1893, Chicago"],["Fastest roller coaster","Formula Rossa, about 240 km/h"]],
d:[["1583","Bakken opens in Denmark"],["1843","Tivoli Gardens opens"],["1893","First Ferris wheel in Chicago"],["1895","Coney Island’s first enclosed park"],["1955","Disneyland opens"],["1971","Walt Disney World opens"],["1983","Tokyo Disneyland, first Disney park outside the USA"]],
s2:[["Why we enjoy thrill rides","Roller coasters trigger the body’s fight-or-flight response, releasing adrenaline, while our brains know we are safe. This mix of fear and safety creates excitement, and many riders feel happy and energized afterward."],["Behind the scenes","Large theme parks have hidden tunnels, called utilidors at Walt Disney World, that let workers move around unseen. Parks also manage huge crowds using queue designs, virtual queues and smartphone apps."]],
sp:["Tivoli’s influence","Walt Disney visited Copenhagen’s Tivoli Gardens in the early 1950s and admired its cleanliness, lights, gardens and friendly atmosphere. He said it helped inspire his plans for Disneyland."],
g:[["Theme park","An amusement park built around themes or stories"],["Animatronics","Robotic figures that move and talk"],["Adrenaline","A hormone released in exciting or scary moments"],["Attraction","A ride, show or feature that draws visitors"]],
f:["Bakken in Denmark has operated since 1583.","Walt Disney was inspired partly by Tivoli Gardens.","The first Ferris wheel was built for the 1893 Chicago world’s fair."],
fun:"Disneyland’s Sleeping Beauty Castle is only about 23 meters tall, but it uses forced perspective, with smaller details higher up, to look much taller.",
tl:"Ang theme park ay pasyalang may mga sakayan at atraksiyong batay sa isang tema o kuwento. Binago ng Disneyland noong 1955 ang mga theme park, at ngayon ay dinarayo ito ng sampu-sampung milyong bisita taun-taon."});

ADDART({id:"airpollution",t:"Air Pollution",c:"The Modern World",i:"😷",w:"Air pollution",r:["pollution","lungs","climchange","fossilfuels","coal"],
p:[["Dirty air","Air pollution is harmful gases and particles in the air we breathe."],["A major killer","The WHO links air pollution to about 7 million early deaths a year."],["Cleaner air is possible","Laws and cleaner technology have improved air quality in many cities."]],
l:"Air pollution is the presence in the air of substances that harm human health and the environment. The most dangerous pollutants include fine particles called PM2.5, small enough to enter the lungs and bloodstream; nitrogen dioxide, mainly from traffic; ozone formed near the ground; sulfur dioxide from burning coal; and carbon monoxide. Sources include vehicles, power stations, factories, burning crops and waste, wildfires, dust storms and, in many homes, cooking and heating with wood, coal or kerosene. The World Health Organization estimates that air pollution, outdoors and indoors, causes about 7 million premature deaths each year from stroke, heart disease, lung cancer and lung diseases, and that 99 percent of people breathe air exceeding its guidelines. The Great Smog of London in 1952 led to the first clean air laws, and cities from Los Angeles to Beijing have since cut pollution with stricter rules and cleaner technology, though many cities in South Asia and Africa still suffer severe smog.",
s:[["Main pollutants","PM2.5 particles, smaller than a thirtieth of the width of a human hair, are considered the most harmful. Nitrogen dioxide from diesel engines irritates airways and worsens asthma. Ground-level ozone, formed when sunlight acts on pollution, causes summer smog. Indoor smoke from cooking on open fires mainly harms women and children in poorer countries."],["The Great Smog","In December 1952, cold, still weather trapped coal smoke over London for five days. Visibility fell to a few meters, and the smog caused about 4,000 deaths at the time and perhaps 10,000 or more in the following months. Britain passed the Clean Air Act in 1956, and the United States its major Clean Air Act in 1970."],["Cleaning the air","Catalytic converters on cars, removing lead from gasoline, scrubbers on power stations and switching from coal to cleaner energy have reduced pollution in many countries. Beijing cut PM2.5 levels by more than half between 2013 and 2023 through strict measures. Low-emission zones limit polluting vehicles in many cities."],["Air pollution and climate","Many sources of air pollution, such as burning fossil fuels, also release greenhouse gases, so cutting them helps both health and climate. Wildfires made worse by climate change can spread smoke across whole continents, as in North America in 2023."]],
k:[["Early deaths per year (WHO)","About 7 million"],["People breathing unsafe air","About 99%"],["Most harmful pollutant","Fine particles (PM2.5)"],["Great Smog of London","December 1952"],["UK Clean Air Act","1956"]],
d:[["1952","Great Smog of London"],["1956","UK Clean Air Act"],["1970","US Clean Air Act"],["1975","Catalytic converters introduced on US cars"],["2013","Beijing’s ‘airpocalypse’ leads to a national clean air plan"],["2021","WHO tightens air quality guidelines"]],
s2:[["Measuring air quality","Air quality indexes turn measurements of pollutants into simple color-coded scales, from green for good to purple or maroon for hazardous. Cheap sensors and phone apps now let people check local air quality in real time."],["Protecting yourself","On high-pollution days, health agencies advise reducing strenuous outdoor exercise, keeping windows closed and using air purifiers if possible. Well-fitting masks rated for fine particles can reduce exposure."]],
sp:["The lead story","Adding lead to gasoline to improve engine performance, from the 1920s, spread toxic lead around the world, harming children’s brains. After decades of campaigns, the last country to stop selling leaded gasoline for cars, Algeria, did so in 2021, which the UN called a major public health victory."],
g:[["Pollutant","A harmful substance in the environment"],["PM2.5","Fine particles less than 2.5 micrometers across"],["Smog","Smoke and fog, or haze from air pollution"],["Catalytic converter","A device that cleans car exhaust"]],
f:["About 99 percent of people breathe air that exceeds WHO guidelines.","The Great Smog of 1952 killed thousands of Londoners.","Leaded gasoline for cars was finally phased out worldwide in 2021."],
fun:"The word ‘smog’, combining smoke and fog, was popularized in 1905 by a London doctor, Henry Antoine Des Voeux.",
tl:"Ang polusyon sa hangin ay mga mapanganib na gas at partikulo sa hanging nilalanghap natin. Iniuugnay ito ng WHO sa mga 7 milyong maagang kamatayan bawat taon, ngunit napabuti ng mga batas at malinis na teknolohiya ang hangin sa maraming lungsod."});

ADDART({id:"minimumwage",t:"The Minimum Wage",c:"The Modern World",i:"💵",w:"Minimum wage",r:["inequality","costliving","gigeconomy","labor","inflation"],
p:[["A legal pay floor","A minimum wage is the lowest pay employers are allowed to give workers."],["Over a century old","New Zealand and Australia introduced the first minimum wages in the 1890s."],["Much debated","Economists disagree about its effects on jobs and poverty."]],
l:"A minimum wage is the lowest hourly, daily or monthly pay that employers are legally allowed to pay workers. Its aims are to protect low-paid workers from exploitation, reduce poverty and inequality, and ensure that work provides a decent living. New Zealand introduced the first national system in 1894, followed by Australia, where a 1907 court case set a ‘fair and reasonable’ basic wage. The United States set a federal minimum wage of 25 cents an hour in 1938, Britain introduced a national minimum wage in 1999 and Germany in 2015. Today about 90 percent of countries have some form of minimum wage, though levels vary hugely, and many workers in informal jobs are not covered. Supporters argue that higher minimum wages raise living standards and spending; critics worry they can cost jobs, especially for young and less-skilled workers. Research since the 1990s suggests moderate increases have small effects on employment, while large increases remain debated.",
s:[["History","Rapid industrialization led to ‘sweated’ labor, with long hours and very low pay, especially for women and children. New Zealand’s Industrial Conciliation and Arbitration Act of 1894 allowed boards to set minimum pay. In Australia, Justice Henry Higgins’s 1907 Harvester judgment said wages should support a worker and family in ‘frugal comfort’."],["How it is set","Some countries set minimum wages by law; others use independent commissions or negotiations with unions and employers. Many adjust them regularly for inflation. Some have different rates for young people, regions or industries. In countries without a legal minimum, such as some Nordic nations, unions negotiate wage floors."],["The economic debate","Basic economic theory predicts that raising the price of labor above its market level reduces employment. In 1994, economists David Card and Alan Krueger compared fast-food jobs in New Jersey, which raised its minimum wage, with neighboring Pennsylvania and found no job losses. Card shared the 2021 Nobel Prize in Economics partly for this work. Other studies find some job losses, particularly with large increases."],["Living wage","A living wage is an estimate of what a worker needs to afford basic needs such as food, housing and transport where they live. Campaigns encourage employers to pay this voluntarily, and some governments use it as a guide. Living wage levels are usually higher than legal minimum wages."]],
k:[["First national system","New Zealand, 1894"],["US federal minimum introduced","1938, 25 cents an hour"],["UK national minimum wage","1999"],["Germany","2015"],["Countries with a minimum wage","About 90%"]],
d:[["1894","New Zealand introduces minimum pay rules"],["1907","Australia’s Harvester judgment"],["1938","US Fair Labor Standards Act"],["1970","ILO Minimum Wage Fixing Convention"],["1994","Card and Krueger study"],["1999","UK national minimum wage"],["2015","Germany introduces a minimum wage"]],
s2:[["Who earns the minimum wage?","Minimum wage workers often work in retail, hospitality, cleaning, care and farming. Many are young, but in many countries a large share are adults supporting families, and women are often overrepresented."],["Minimum wage and robots","Some argue that higher wages encourage companies to use machines, such as self-service checkouts, instead of workers. Others say businesses automate anyway and that higher wages reduce staff turnover and improve productivity."]],
sp:["The Harvester judgment","In 1907 the Sunshine Harvester factory in Melbourne, maker of farm machinery, applied for a tax exemption that depended on paying ‘fair and reasonable’ wages. Justice Higgins studied family budgets to decide what that meant, setting a basic wage of seven shillings a day."],
g:[["Minimum wage","The lowest pay allowed by law"],["Living wage","Pay needed to cover basic living costs"],["Informal job","Work not registered or protected by labor laws"],["Inflation","A general rise in prices over time"]],
f:["New Zealand introduced the first national minimum wage system.","The first US federal minimum wage was 25 cents an hour.","An economist won a Nobel Prize partly for studying minimum wages."],
fun:"US President Franklin D. Roosevelt said in 1933 that no business that depends on paying workers less than a living wage has any right to continue.",
tl:"Ang minimum wage ay pinakamababang sahod na pinapayagang ibigay ng employer sa manggagawa. Nagsimula ito sa New Zealand noong 1894, at pinagtatalunan ng mga ekonomista ang epekto nito sa trabaho at kahirapan."});
